    "https://tru.hua.hrsmart.com/hr/ats/Posting/view/35522": {
      "title": "Sessional - Faculty EDUC 5990 - (02050.11997)",
      "description": "Jump to main content Attention Safari users: This site requires cookies to function. Please click here to accept the cookie. Cookies will be used strictly for the functioning of the site. Toggle navigation Job Search View All Jobs Create Account Log in Help Help on this Page Job Details Sessional - Faculty EDUC 5990 - (02050.11997) Share this job as a link in your status update to LinkedIn. Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Faculty (Sessional) Posting In effect from 23/9/2026 Closing Application Date 8/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Instructional Type Academic/Career(max 24 credits) Anticipated Start Date 20/10/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) 10/12/2026 DUTIES OVERVIEW Thompson Rivers University, in partnership with the Nicola Valley Institute of Technology (NVIT), is delivering a select cohort of the Master of Education program to members of the St’uxwtéws (Bonaparte First Nation) at the NVIT campus in Merritt, BC. RESPONSIBILITIES Thompson Rivers University is seeking sessional faculty member(s) for this select cohort of the Master of Education (MEd) program to plan and deliver 2 sections of EDUC 5990 – Indigenous Pedagogies. Each 3-credit graduate course involves 39 to 42 hours of direct instruction and will be delivered 50% synchronous online and 50% in-person at the Nicola Valley Institute of Technology (NVIT) campus in Merritt, BC. COURSE DESCRIPTION EDUC 5990 Indigenous Pedagogies (Fall 2026) In this course, students will engage with an interdisciplinary approach to understanding Indigenous pedagogies. Critical analysis of impact in incorporating Indigenous ways of knowing and being into education will be examined along with exploration of traditional and contemporary approaches to problem-solving via multiple worldviews. An opportunity for synthesis of self-awareness, theoretical knowledge, experience, and skills will be provided to enhance personal and professional growth. Students will collectively and individually explore, challenge, and identify implications for practice to promote culturally safe, anti-racist, anti-oppressive, and equity-focused environments. Students will share their experiences and engage in dialogue with each other. QUALIFICATIONS A PhD or EdD is normally required; however, equivalent qualifications and/or experience will be considered, such as professional qualifications or where the candidate has accumulated experience and accomplishments judged to be particularly relevant and valuable. Examples of experience and accomplishments of relevance might include Indigenous knowledge in graduate programs or teaching Indigenous ways of knowing in the K-12 environment. One degree in education is required, with a Bachelor of Education or equivalent strongly preferred. Demonstrated successful post-secondary teaching in a teacher education program is preferred. Demonstrated expertise in educational leadership and learning in Indigenous contexts is strongly preferred. Self-identification as an Indigenous person (e.g., First Nations, Inuit, or Métis) and demonstrated knowledge of Indigenous worldviews, histories, cultures, contemporary realities, and experience building respectful, reciprocal relationships with Indigenous communities, as well as experience working in community is required. Demonstrated experience in integrating Indigenous knowledge and teaching methods is required (TRC CA 62). Proven skills in building student capacity for intercultural understanding, empathy, and mutual respect is required (TRC CA 63). Teaching excellence, including developing culturally appropriate curricula is required (TRC CA 10). Excellent organizational, interpersonal, and communication skills is required. Evidence of substantial and recent engagement in educational leadership in Indigenous contexts through peer-reviewed dissemination of research and/or the scholarship of teaching is strongly preferred. Pay Band $2,732.34 - $2,732.34 ( Sessional Faculty - Academic (per credit) ) Location BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 2 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job v18.5.4 Last sync: 2026-09-24 03:55:04 Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Faculty (Sessional) Posting In effect from 23/9/2026 Closing Application Date 8/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Instructional Type Academic/Career(max 24 credits) Anticipated Start Date 20/10/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) 10/12/2026 DUTIES OVERVIEW Thompson Rivers University, in partnership with the Nicola Valley Institute of Technology (NVIT), is delivering a select cohort of the Master of Education program to members of the St’uxwtéws (Bonaparte First Nation) at the NVIT campus in Merritt, BC. RESPONSIBILITIES Thompson Rivers University is seeking sessional faculty member(s) for this select cohort of the Master of Education (MEd) program to plan and deliver 2 sections of EDUC 5990 – Indigenous Pedagogies. Each 3-credit graduate course involves 39 to 42 hours of direct instruction and will be delivered 50% synchronous online and 50% in-person at the Nicola Valley Institute of Technology (NVIT) campus in Merritt, BC. COURSE DESCRIPTION EDUC 5990 Indigenous Pedagogies (Fall 2026) In this course, students will engage with an interdisciplinary approach to understanding Indigenous pedagogies. Critical analysis of impact in incorporating Indigenous ways of knowing and being into education will be examined along with exploration of traditional and contemporary approaches to problem-solving via multiple worldviews. An opportunity for synthesis of self-awareness, theoretical knowledge, experience, and skills will be provided to enhance personal and professional growth. Students will collectively and individually explore, challenge, and identify implications for practice to promote culturally safe, anti-racist, anti-oppressive, and equity-focused environments. Students will share their experiences and engage in dialogue with each other. QUALIFICATIONS A PhD or EdD is normally required; however, equivalent qualifications and/or experience will be considered, such as professional qualifications or where the candidate has accumulated experience and accomplishments judged to be particularly relevant and valuable. Examples of experience and accomplishments of relevance might include Indigenous knowledge in graduate programs or teaching Indigenous ways of knowing in the K-12 environment. One degree in education is required, with a Bachelor of Education or equivalent strongly preferred. Demonstrated successful post-secondary teaching in a teacher education program is preferred. Demonstrated expertise in educational leadership and learning in Indigenous contexts is strongly preferred. Self-identification as an Indigenous person (e.g., First Nations, Inuit, or Métis) and demonstrated knowledge of Indigenous worldviews, histories, cultures, contemporary realities, and experience building respectful, reciprocal relationships with Indigenous communities, as well as experience working in community is required. Demonstrated experience in integrating Indigenous knowledge and teaching methods is required (TRC CA 62). Proven skills in building student capacity for intercultural understanding, empathy, and mutual respect is required (TRC CA 63). Teaching excellence, including developing culturally appropriate curricula is required (TRC CA 10). Excellent organizational, interpersonal, and communication skills is required. Evidence of substantial and recent engagement in educational leadership in Indigenous contexts through peer-reviewed dissemination of research and/or the scholarship of teaching is strongly preferred. Pay Band $2,732.34 - $2,732.34 ( Sessional Faculty - Academic (per credit) ) Location BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 2 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job"
